prompt:
Read, in crates/spawn-core/src/bridge.rs, `input()`, `type_once()`, `typing_of()`, `step()`, `stop()` and the end flow (`end`), `PANE_NOT_READY`, `MAY_HAVE_TYPED` and `BridgeConfig` (`ready_attempts`, `input_attempts`); `CommandStep` and `EventKind::CommandStepped` in crates/spawn-core/src/event.rs; how a `typed` step consumes a ready screen in crates/spawn-core/src/observe/snapshot.rs; `spawn_claim` and `spawn_finish` in crates/spawn-postgres/sql/schema.sql (one command per session at a time, in id order); in crates/spawn-cli/src/deliver.rs the delivery attempts and their retries (`attempts`, `delivered`, the `deliver-<task>-<n>` keys and their `-retry-<k>` successors) and `HumanNote` (a human's command is news once it finished); `DISPATCH_FACTS` and `UNTOLD_MERGES` in crates/spawn-postgres/src/fleet.rs; the facts the stall layer counts as activity in crates/spawn-cli/src/dispatch/stall.rs; docs/design.md §5 (the `Input` row and the at-most-once rule) and §14 (the causality invariant), and docs/fleet-architecture.md §3.

The problem. An input to a session whose engine is in the middle of a turn is refused at once and lost. `spawn submit --action '{"type":"input",...}'` to a worker mid-turn finishes failed with `pane state Busy does not accept input`; nothing types it later, and the sender has to notice and send it again by hand. On 2026-11-06 the owner had to loop three times to deliver one note to task 45's worker, each time racing the worker's next turn. The supervisor's notes to workers and a human's messages from the board fail the same way. The refusal itself is right (text typed into a running turn would be lost or would derail it); dropping the input is not. The dispatcher already copes on its own (it retries a refused delivery 60, 120 and 240 s later and the stall layer reads those failures), so it is not the dispatcher's inputs that need this.

Required behaviour (the contract the gate checks). An input that cannot be typed yet waits for its turn.
1. Who waits. An `Input` command from any principal other than Spawn's own loops waits: `human`, the supervisor (`spawn_supervisor`, through the gateway), and any other principal of `spawn submit`. Inputs of `spawn-dispatch`, `spawn-integrate` and `spawn-liveness` keep today's contract exactly: refused at once with `pane state <state> does not accept input` (`PANE_NOT_READY`) when the pane is not ready, so the dispatcher's retry schedule, its `deliver-*` keys and the stall evidence built on those failures stay as they are. An input to a session that runs no execution still fails at once, as today (it does not wait for a start).
2. Recorded as waiting. When the Bridge reads the pane as not ready for an input that waits (Busy, Drafted, Permission, Error, Unknown, or a capture that keeps failing on a live terminal), it records one `command.step` with the new step `waiting` for that command (`CommandStep::Waiting`, wire name `waiting`, keyed `<command>:waiting`, so a retried claim or a restarted Bridge records it once), and leaves the command unfinished. Nothing is typed and no `typing` step is written. The waiting is read from the log, not held in the Bridge's memory: a restarted Bridge (a restarted `spawn loop`) carries on with every unfinished input that has a `waiting` step.
3. Typed when the turn ends, by the ready rules the Bridge already uses: a reading of `Ready` (`ScreenState::accepts_input`, the same `read_claude_screen` and the same staging and `typing`/`typed` steps as today). A ready reading is consumed by the input typed on it: the next input of the session is typed only on a Ready reading taken after the previous input's `typed` record (the rule the Observer's snapshot already applies), so two inputs never land in one prompt.
4. In order, at most once. A session's waiting inputs are typed oldest first (command id order), and no input of the session, waiting or not, from any principal, is typed before an earlier input of the session that still waits. The at-most-once rule of §5 holds unchanged: a waiting input with a `typed` step succeeds without typing again; one with `typing` and no `typed` fails with `MAY_HAVE_TYPED` and is never typed again.
5. Not a wall. A waiting input does not hold the session's other commands: `stop`, `discard` and `resume` behind it are claimed and handled at once (today `spawn_claim` would hold them behind the unfinished input; change it, for example by letting a command pass unfinished inputs that have a `waiting` step, but never another input). Inputs of Spawn's loops behind it are claimed too and, by rule 4, refused at once as not ready while it waits.
6. Ends first. When the session's execution ends before a waiting input is typed, the input finishes failed, never typed, with an error that names why, recorded in the same tick as the end or the next: `the session was stopped before the input was typed` for a `stop` or `discard` (the word `stopped` is what the gate checks), and `the session's execution ended (<cause>) before the input was typed` for an engine that exited or a terminal that was lost. A later `resume` does not revive it: the sender decides whether to send it again, and the human or supervisor news item (`HumanNote`) says it failed and why, as it does for any failed command.
7. No timeout of its own. A turn may run for an hour; the input waits as long as the session's execution lives. The lease is not the bound: a command that waits keeps waiting whatever its lease does (renew it, or release it and keep the `waiting` record; say which in the module comment). The input still shows as unfinished (`pending`) in `spawn_commands`; the fleet and session pages may show it as waiting later (out of scope here).
8. Causality (§14). The `waiting` step is the record that the Bridge holds the input; the typing, the success and the failure are each recorded outcomes the sender can read, so no one has to infer delivery from timing. The `waiting` record is not the worker's activity: dispatch/stall.rs does not count it as a fact that moves a task's `activity` step, and it does not make the session's judgement look stale to the dispatcher (treat it as the Observer treats the session's other command steps, and test that).
State the rule in the `Input` row of docs/design.md §5 (and the at-most-once paragraph), in docs/fleet-architecture.md §3 (inputs from people and the supervisor wait, Spawn's own inputs are refused and retried), and in the module comment of bridge.rs. Add unit tests (with the in-memory store and a fake terminal, as the Bridge's tests do):
- an input to a Busy pane is recorded `waiting` once, unfinished, nothing typed; typed and succeeded once the pane reads Ready;
- two waiting inputs are typed oldest first, each on its own Ready reading;
- a dispatcher input to a Busy pane still fails at once with `PANE_NOT_READY`, and one behind a waiting input is refused, not typed first;
- a `stop` behind a waiting input is claimed and handled; the input fails with `stopped`; an engine exit fails it with the cause;
- a Bridge rebuilt from the log keeps waiting and types once; a waiting input with `typing` and no `typed` fails with `MAY_HAVE_TYPED`;
- in crates/spawn-postgres/tests/, `spawn_claim` hands out a `stop` behind an unfinished input that has a `waiting` step, and still not an input behind it;
- dispatch/stall.rs: a `waiting` record does not move the `activity` step.

The gate judges this with rsi/scenarios/015.sh (read it). A real `spawn loop` (no model judge, no supervisor) runs one resident, `echo`, on a fake engine whose turns show Claude Code's spinner (`✻ Churning… (esc to interrupt)`, read as Busy) above the prompt for as long as they run, and which logs every line it reads and flags any line typed while a turn runs. The scenario sends inputs with `spawn submit` as `human` and as `spawn_supervisor`:
- (1) two inputs sent while a 45 s turn runs are not finished while it runs and each has exactly one `waiting` step from the Bridge; nothing reached the engine;
- (2) the loop is restarted while they wait; after the turn ends both succeed, each with one `typed` step, the engine reads each exactly once, the first after the long turn ended and the second after the first's own turn ended; nothing is ever typed into a busy pane;
- (3) an input waits behind a 120 s turn, then a `stop` of the session is handled within 60 s while the turn still runs, and the input finishes failed with an error containing `stopped`, with no `typing` step, never read by the engine;
- (4) after a `resume`, a task assigned to the resident while a human's input waits behind another turn is delivered by the dispatcher exactly once, after that input; every failed attempt of its delivery failed as `does not accept input`, and no input of `spawn-dispatch` is ever recorded `waiting`.
On the live main (115f078; the deployed binary is 3e2c08e) it fails at (1): "input 3, sent while the session's turn ran, finished finished:false (pane state Busy does not accept input) instead of waiting for the turn to end". It exits 0 when every check passes, 1 when an assertion fails, 2 on a harness failure.

Rules: never edit the protected paths, which the gate rejects: rsi/, scripts/ (scripts/rsi/, scripts/blackbox/, scripts/e2e.sh among them), deploy/, all of web/ except web/src/ and web/index.html, rust-toolchain files and .cargo/. The gate also rejects fewer runnable tests than the base (do not delete, rename, ignore or disable tests), any lint relaxation ([lints] tables, clippy.toml, a new crate-level #![allow]/#![expect], a removed #![warn]/#![deny]) and any build-time code (build.rs, .cargo/, a `build =` key in a Cargo.toml). The gate builds offline from the cargo caches already on the host: a new crate builds only if it is already in ~/.cargo/registry. Keep every existing check green (clippy -D warnings, the workspace tests, the e2e, the black-box scenarios, the web build) and add tests of your own for the new behaviour.
